   "stem": {
      "text": "Evaluate the improper integral \\( \\int_{2}^{\\infty} \\frac{8 x}{\\left(x^{2} + 7\\right)^{2}}\\,dx \\), or show that it diverges.",
      "command_verb": "evaluate"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "statement",
      "label": "The integral converges to \\( \\frac{4}{11} \\)."
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The integral is improper because the upper limit of integration is infinite, so write it as \\( \\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\int_{2}^{R} \\frac{8 x}{\\left(x^{2} + 7\\right)^{2}}\\,dx \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99053",
         "rule_named": "improper integral as a limit"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Substitute \\( u = x^{2} + 7,\\ du = 2 x\\,dx \\). The limits become \\( u = 11 \\text{ to } u = R^{2} + 7 \\), and an antiderivative in u is \\( - \\frac{4}{u} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99003",
         "rule_named": "substitution with changed limits"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluate the limit: \\( \\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\left( - \\frac{4}{R^{2} + 7} + \\frac{4}{11} \\right) = \\frac{4}{11} \\). The limit is finite, so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{4}{11} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99005",
         "rule_named": "limit of the definite integrals"
      }
   ],
